{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789505296,"nonce":"kMXTxX6L79Zoy7jSaeJu9UT/Uqg=","title":"Python: a typed list handed to a bound extend record crashes the process","message":"`apply(xs.extend, [2])` with `def apply(f, v): f(v)` dies with a signal (CPython prints [2]). The record's adapter reads its argument back with `zb_list_unbox_any`, which accepts only a box carrying a list of dynamic values; `[2]` is boxed as a list of ints, so the unbox raises TypeError and hands back a null header, and `zb_list_extend_any` reads the length through it. Two defects: `coerce` from a dynamic value to `List(Object)` should take any list kind (converting by tag as `zb_any_iter` does, or better keeping the storage once lists widen, 202606d), and the unbox is not guarded the way a primitive read is, so a raised TypeError is followed by a use of the null instead of the pending check.\n\nSame shape with a value assigned in two branches, `f = xs.extend` in each, then `f([2])`: the binding is not one the direct path takes, so the record path runs.","files":null}]}